A||||60||the need for speed||9mm cartridge life expectancy||||1||
Z||000000||the need for speed||12-18-2001||07:44 PM||||just wondering how long you should hold on to bullets?I have had my hand gun now for about 7 years I only use fmj bullets for practice but I keep a clip of hollow tip bullets at home for self defense, I keep cycling the bullets  through a couple of clips for longer life of the clip.the question is should I use them as target practices and replace them or are they fine to hold on to tell they are needed?<P><BR> just wanted to edit one more time for bn boomer!!<BR>[This message has been edited by the need for speed (edited 12-21-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by the need for speed (edited 12-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||12-18-2001||07:50 PM||||I understand that you still can buy some surpluss ammo from WWII. As long as you have it stored in a dry, cool place it will last a long time. I have shot some ammo that was over twenty years old, some did missfire. If you want to be safe use it up and get some fresh ammo. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||reddog||12-18-2001||08:12 PM||||Hard to say. Depends on alot of different factors. BTW, I used to be a Firearms instructor for a couple different Law Enforcement agencies and I always told my students to replace them at least once a year or sooner. Not that they would go bad that soon but it's worth the extra money to replace them and use your old rounds for practice. Better safe than sorry. ( Just a note: the  older the ammo the more likely that you will get either a misfire or a hangfire which is much more dagerous!!!)<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||WampusCat||12-18-2001||08:22 PM||||If ammo is in a box in my gun safe with dehumidifier on, I'd have no problem keeping it for years and years.  In the magazine or chamber of a pistol, it has a chance for oil or solvent to make it's way into the primer.  I fire off any rounds that have been in a gun more than a year, which kinda sux, because I'm running out of Black Talons and I can't get them anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000004||REDHOTT88||12-18-2001||10:45 PM||||                                              EDITED: There's enough "experts" here.    <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/wpn.gif">                                <BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">              <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||12-19-2001||10:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>I'm running out of Black Talons and I can't get them anymore.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah...gotta have more humane bullets.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><BR>Gotta love those Black Talons!||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-19-2001||08:45 PM||||You've had this gun for over 7 years and haven't used up one lousy box in it.... You're more danger to yourself than the criminal. Any one seriously planning to use a gun as a self defence tool needs to shoot it regularly. At least 50-100 rounds every couple months. Better yet, find an NRA certified instructor. Most good gun clubs have at least one NRA rated instructor. Can't find a club? <A HREF="http://www.nra.org" TARGET=_blank>www.nra.org</A> <P>You should probably disassemble the gun and clean/lube everything BEFORE you think about shooting it. If it's been setting around for ages god only knows how much crap might have gotten into it. If you are uncomfortable with that then have it done at a local shop. Most good shops will show you how to do proper cleanings. Some may charge you for the lesson.<P>My father stored one of my guns for years. After he died and I retrieved it, it was so full of crap from tobaco and wood smoke the thing couldn't fire. You'd pull the trigger and maybe it click and maybe it don't. The bolt had to be completely stipped and soaked in an industrial solvent to get all the crap out.<P>The ammo is likely still good unless it was baddly stored. Modern ammo is really durable. Once the gun is thuroghly checked over, shoot it, buy another box and shoot that. If it was bought new, it isn't even broken in yet. No way I'd trust it without putting at least a couple hundred rounds thru it.<P>You can buy 9mm amo in bulk from any number of mail order suppliers. Buy Plenty, Practice Often. Check <A HREF="http://www.cheaperthandirt.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cheaperthandirt.com/</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.sportsmansguide.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sportsmansguide.com/</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||12-20-2001||02:34 AM||||theogre: You have got to be joking, or do not know anything about firearms.. THere is nothign wrong with a gun that has been sitting for 40 years, let alone a few months! If it's 40 years old, it may need an inspection to make sure the barrel and action aren't rusty, but besides that, they are fine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||12-20-2001||03:19 AM||||Agreed 110%.   Clean the gun, dump the old ammo, and start going to the range. Regularily.  <P>IF YOU ARE NOT WILLING TO DO THIS, THEN YOU SHOULD NOT BE CARRYING! Put the gun down.<P>I would not keep ammo I expect to need for over 1 year.    Test rounds maybe 2 years, tops. (NEVER STORE IT IN THE GUN!  Dispose of in-gun rounds within a month.)  These are all conservative times, but you won't die by them.<P>A hung fire can KILL you even after you've cleared it from the gun and set it on the table, or thrown it in the trash...<P>You can take the old ammo to a gun shop, and they can dispose of it properly withought ever firing it.  Get a new box for $20.  Much cheaper than a coffin.<P>Be Safe. Please.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>You've had this gun for over 7 years and haven't used up one lousy box in it.... You're more danger to yourself than the criminal.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||dennis_6||12-20-2001||03:26 AM||||JohnnyK - Have you ever heard of pitting?  How about having your barrel blocked?  A gun can start to rust in a very short amount of time.  It could dangerously rust in a few months.  I guess a 40 year old Fiero would start up fine with no problems.  A lot of cars have problems after sitting two weeks.<p>[This message has been edited by dennis_6 (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||12-20-2001||03:27 AM||||edited because I'm a dumearse<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000011||reddog||12-20-2001||03:39 AM||||Hey lets not forget a squib load! Thats when you pull the trigger and the gun fires but not as loud as usual. Meaning only the primer fired giving the round just enough velocity to travel out of the casing and into the chamber bore or barrel. Most people just think it is a misfire and eject the casing and reload and shot. The next thing you know your weapon is blowing up in your hand not a pretty picture. At the least you have lost a $300.-$700. handgun. And it could be much worse. You could be nicknamed "lefty" for the rest of your life. BTW Brandon Lee was killed by a sqiub load. The stuck round was left in the prop gun and later a blank was fired unsticking the round and sending it into Brandon's abdomin. My point being their are a lot of possible accidents to be had if you are using substandard or out of date equipment. Be sure to be safe and practice frequeantly. And as the others have said, find a competant instructor to teach you!<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||reddog||12-20-2001||03:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dennis_6:<BR><B>JohnnyK - Have you ever heard of pitting?  How about having your barrel blocked?  A gun can start to rust in a very short amount of time.  It could dangerously rust in a few months.  I guess a 40 year old Fiero would start up fine with no problems.  A lot of cars have problems after sitting two weeks.<P>Dennis: buy a Glock & That kind of $hit never happens.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>[This message has been edited by dennis_6 (edited 12-20-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||the need for speed||12-20-2001||11:41 AM||||if you shoot guns regularly [theorge] you would know not to use expensive hollow tip bullets as target practice, pay attention if your going to comment on posts.I thought your first paragraph was not even relevant both of you [theorge and artherd]all that was need was one question answered not the need for a daddy reply syndrome!If you reply to every ones posts as rudely as this please do not bother replying to any more of mine!           politeness counts.||||reg||1
Z||000014||REDHOTT88||12-20-2001||12:18 PM||||EDIT......making room for the "experts"     <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/shootout.gif">  <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||12-20-2001||04:08 PM||||Actually I do shoot. Used to shoot allot except there's no range available that's 200+ yards long around here. I shoot mainly rifles. I generally find handguns tedious and boring. There are a couple competitive classes that are interesting for handgun but they aren't readily available in my area.<P>Don't use "expensive" hollow points for practice? What good are they sitting around for 7 years? Odds good they all should fire, but any fool that trusts ammo that old for a defence gun deserves whatever happens to him. If you think a box of 9mm is expensive, try rifle ammo, which commonly runs $10-20 or more per box of 20 depending on what load it is. The two links I provided above stock 9mm like it was candy, as little as $6-8 per box of 50. Don't whine to me about ammo prices. <P>The only reason not to shoot them off is that some shooting ranges won't let you shoot certain ammo types because it's hard on their backstops or they are trying to control lead contamination. (The EPA has been increasingly riding ranges about that.)<P>Glock? Doesn't have such problems? Glock still uses a metal barrel and other metal parts. Those parts are subject to the same problems as any other gun.<P>Store a gun 40 years? Then pick it up and shoot it? Not any where near me you won't. Smoke and Dust contamination can accumulate even in guns kept in lockers. Moisture will permiate most "air tight" containers. Unless properly stored all guns are subject to corrosion and lubrication gumming over time. Many times a corrosion/lube problem shows up as a hung firing pin after pulling the trigger. It may even fire a few before it hangs. Maybe then it drops as you try to extract a round. I've seen these types of problems on more than a few guns over the years. Why do you think the U.S. military specially packs guns prior to long term storage? The fun of it?<P>A miss fire of any type <u>in the gun</u> is extremely dangerous. Doesn't matter if it's the gun or the ammo's fault. Only god knows if or when it might go.<P>A miss fired round, <u>once it is out of the gun</u>, is dangerous but not as bad as you might think. It's essentially the same issue as when stored ammo is caught in a fire. It pops and makes noise but because the round doesn't have enough support, it can't "shoot" with much energy. (Ammo in fires was tested by by the NRA's American Rifleman a few years ago.) <P>When a round is in the gun, the brass can swell only enough to seal the chamber. The entire force of the burning charge is trapped behind the round, squirting it up the barrel.<P>When sitting loose, the brass expands uncontrolled and once the bullet pops from the casing, the burning charge expands in every direction instead of pushing the round.<P>That doesn't mean I want to be next to a loose round when it pops. It could injure you but at least it's not likely to be fatal if a piece does hit someone.<P>I'll have to dig around and see if I still have that issue of American Rifleman. It was a very interesting read.<P>Need For Speed, People with your additude are one of the reasons clowns like Rosey O'Donnel, with her "Million Mom" bunch, and Handgun Control Inc. gain more ground in destroying the rights of responcible gun owners. What are you going to tell us next? that you store it unlocked in the nightstand.... ||||reg||1
Z||000016||REDHOTT88||12-20-2001||04:11 PM||||What the Ogre said...    <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/up.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||BN Boomer||12-20-2001||04:26 PM||||Most cops cycle their ammunition at least once a year. If you are using your weapon as a defensive tool and staking your life upon its performance, it only makes sense to eliminate the chance of failure due to faulty ammunition as much as possible by using fresh ammo. Use up the old stuff and get some new ammunition. For 9mm, I'd look at Cor-Bon or Federal HydrShock.<P>I have fired over 50 year old WWII surplus USGI 30-06, .30 Carbine, and .45 ACP with no failures or adverse affects. I have also fired 10 year old cheap commercial .22LR and experienced a high rate of mis-fires. In my opinion, the high quality ammo seems to last almost indefinitely under the proper storage conditions.<P>Maybe we need to organize a local Fiero owner's shoot. There is a great spot out on the Mountain Loop Highway that is just off the road. I'm on an e-mail list of local AR-15 owners/enthusiasts and we have group get togethers/shoots periodically throught the year.<P>Need for speed, in addition to practicing with inexpensive FMJ loads, you should also be occassionally practicing with your carry loads to make sure that they function reliably in your weapon and are accurate.<P><A HREF="http://www.ar15.com/forums/index.html?board=1" TARGET=_blank>My recommended source for firearm information</A>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Greg, aka Boomer<BR>Red 85 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.gmemd.com/en/locomotive/na_freight/sd70mac/images/sd70mac.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||BN Boomer||12-20-2001||04:36 PM||||Hey ogre (and others), you might check out <A HREF="http://www.ammoman.com" TARGET=_blank>Ammoman.com</A>. There are also tons of other e-tailers of discount ammunition.<P>Also, the effect of ammunition in fires has been well documented in Hatcher's Notebook, authored by Maj Gen Julian S. Hatcher. He spent most of his Army career in the Ordnance Department. The book is well worth the read or at least for use as reference by anyone interested in firearms. It is upon this book that the American Rifleman article was based.||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||12-20-2001||05:38 PM||||Boomer, thanks for the Hatcher reference... I knew the AR article referenced someone but couldn't for the life of me remember his name.||||reg||1
Z||000020||JohnnyK||12-20-2001||06:12 PM||||I'm sorry guys, but try talking to an actual gun collector, not someone who has 5 or so guns.. Ammo is still good after years and years and years... 10-20-30 years..||||reg||1
Z||000021||reddog||12-20-2001||07:01 PM||||No one is disputing that Ammo can be good for years but, If your using it for self defense then I would replace it at least once a year. Do what you want. <P>As for the Glock comment: glocks will not rust as easy as other handguns and are far more durable. after 10 years though every thing needs cleaned , although with glocks tennifer coating it hardly ever collects any rust. But what can I say, I am partial to Glocks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Minimal maintenance and extremely reliable  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Just my 2/100 of a greenback!!<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||BN Boomer||12-20-2001||10:24 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>I'm sorry guys, but try talking to an actual gun collector, not someone who has 5 or so guns.. Ammo is still good after years and years and years... 10-20-30 years..</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Does this qualify as a "collection?<P>  <IMG SRC="http://wsphotofews.excite.com/025/PJ/kP/9w/ZE62854.jpg">  <P>Here's a trio of WWII veterans circa 1943. You can be sure that I didn't simply pick any of them up and start shooting them without a thorough examination and cleaning first.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://wsphotofews.excite.com/033/Lb/EY/st/aV57602.jpg">  <P>And my favorite gun of all, the Colt AR15:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://wsphotofews.excite.com/016/qg/ce/yL/Ni99910.jpg">  <P>No one is saying that ammunition is not good for "years and years and years". But just like any other chemical compound, primers and gunpowder do degrade over time. Ammunition stored in hot, humid, or wildly fluctuating conditions accelerates the degradation process, which affects performance. It's one thing to risk or experience a misfire when shooting at that pop can threatening from 100 yards and you can calmly chamber another round, it's an entirely different situation when the target is shooting back or charging you and your gun just goes "click". Degraded components can also drastically affect performance and velocity, even to the point that bullet may not perform as intended. For example, a hollow point may not have sufficient velocity to expand. You'll be hard pressed to find any authority on the defensive use of firearms who would not recommend using up your old ammunition preiodically, at least annually, and replacing it with fresh stock. Ammunition in a carry gun is usually exposed to a wide variety of environments and temperature extremes that can lead to premature degradation of the ammunition components. <P>Which bring us to..... How many guns do YOU own and what makes you an authority on firearms or the defensive application of them?<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000023||donny||12-20-2001||10:48 PM||||Being that my grandfather is a gun collector<BR>and has over 200 guns.I would say I know a little bit about this issue.I myself have had guns all my live and can say that although my grandfather and I will buy military surplus ammo on ocassion we never <BR>rely on it for our self defense weapons.<BR>He keeps a 9mm under his bed and I keep a 380 (locked up of course )in my night stand.<BR>We cycle through our hollow point ammo at least once per year.I myself own an m-1 carbine and sometimes will purchase army surplus ammo but I do sometimes experience <BR>some misfires with it.Anyway to stay more on the topic it's not that the ammo(when stored properly)isn't safe it's that if you heavily rely on your handgun you don't want to take any chances.||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||12-20-2001||11:39 PM||||One last thing..... I'm guessing Need's gun is an automatic.... You are aware that auto's and hollowpoints don't always play well together.... Feed problems are fairly common. Very simply some auto guns and some HP/JHP ammo hate each other. (It's a common enough problem that many gun reviewers will specifically test for it.)<P>Has to do with the shape of the bullets and the fact HP's are often shorter than milball or retail FMJ.<P>Without testing a gun with a particular brand & type of ammo, there's no way to know if this is a problem. Ideally, because of running changes during manufacturing, the test rounds should come from the same batch that will be caried for defence use. But keeping to a tested brand/type is probably sufficient.<P>Old ammo is all over the place.... I have stuff that's 4-10 years old. For target or varmit hunting I'd shoot old ammo all day long. Hunt bear or anything else large with it? No freaking way....||||reg||1
Z||000025||JohnnyK||12-21-2001||01:55 AM||||I'm not going to say anything about any one elses personal hobbies. Lets just say thats not exactly a big collection.<p>[This message has been edited by JohnnyK (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||BN Boomer||12-21-2001||03:36 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>I'm not going to say anything about any one elses personal hobbies. Lets just say thats not exactly a big collection.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Did I say it is a big collection? No. Is it probably consistent with the average collection? Yes. For that matter, did I even say that this was the extent of my collection? <P>First you say don't listen to anyone who only owns 5 or so guns. Now all you can say is that 27 guns from my collection isn't that big. Is there some minimum number of guns one must own before being considered knowledgable about them in your eyes? What is that number?<P>Again, I ask, how many guns do YOU own or upon what is your own supposed expertise in firearms based? What is your firearms background and experience? How much do you shoot? Until you are willing to answer that, maybe it's best if you quit making a fool out of yourself. You haven't offered one shred of proof to back up what you say. Put up or shut up.<P><p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||artherd||12-21-2001||07:10 AM||||BN: NICE AR-15 collection!!!<P>I'm assuming you know, but it is actually legal to own a fully automatic M-16 viariant (or other full-auto weapon) as a private citizen.<P>The only catch is quite a lot of BS and an FBI background check.  And if they find that you're a fellon, a nutcase, or just don't like your socks, they *can* deny you permission.<P>(wheras any 18 year old idoit can walk into wal-mart and buy a frigging .223 single...)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>PS: all you idoits out there, please don't kill anyone I care about.  Thanks.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000028||REDHOTT88||12-21-2001||10:58 AM|||| EDIT..... getting out of the"experts" way.      <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/shot2.gif">                                                                     <BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">             <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000029||WampusCat||12-21-2001||11:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by REDHOTT88:<BR><B>SOOOOOO... the more guns you have, the more you know about fireams?<P>Yeah right, like trying to get to 1000 posts, by posting BS means you know MORE about Fieros!!!<P>I know people who have 1 or 2 guns and know more than most people with collections.<P>Knowledge is in your brain, not your gun quantity in your cabinet!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But how do you get that knowledge in your brain?  By having access to a variety of firearms and shooting, cleaning, and maintaining them on a regular basis.  All the 'knowledge' gained by books or whatever is no substitute for hands on experience under varied conditions.  I think most people who have a fairly large gun collection don't just keep them in a closet or safe but actually enjoy shooting them.  In 13 years in the Marines, I spent many hours firing, disassembling, cleaning and adjusting weapons including M2, M60, M249, M16, M1911, M9, and various .38 revolvers.  I also own several semi-auto pistols and revolvers, several semi-auto, lever action, and bolt action rifles and a couple of shotguns.  I shoot all of them and I've taken most of them apart at least once.  Does this make me an expert?  No, but I consider myself more knowledgeable than the average joe who's maybe shot one or two guns in his life.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||JohnnyK||12-21-2001||12:24 PM||||I was talking to some buddies yesterday about the "replace the shells after a year".. They had a good chuckle..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000031||BN Boomer||12-21-2001||12:48 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by the need for speed:<BR><B>just wondering how long you should hold on to bullets?I have had my hand gun now for about 7 years I only use fmj bullets for practice but I keep a clip of hollow tip bullets at home for self defense, I keep cycling the bullets  through a couple of clips for longer life of the clip.the question is should I use them as target practices and replace them or are they fine to hold on to tell they are needed?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Need for speed, I see that you have now edited your comments a full 3 days after posting them. I know you just wanted to add clarity, but it's frustrating when people do that because it changes the context of some replies. Why not just add a reply of your own clarifying things?<P>At the risk of sounding anal, there is a difference between a "clip" and a "magazine".<P><B>CLIP:</B> <I>A device for holding a group of cartridges.  Typically a Clip is used to charge, load or feed the Magazine.  The terms "Clip and Magazine" are NOT interchangeable in strict technical usage, however semantic wars have been fought over the word, with some insisting it is not a synonym for "magazine."  Like it or not, right or wrong, for 80 years the term CLIP has been used by many gun manufacturers and the USMC. The rest of the military services use the term magazine. There is no argument that it can also mean a separate device for holding and transferring a group of cartridges to a fixed or detachable magazine or as a device inserted with cartridges into the mechanism of a firearm becoming, in effect, part of that mechanism.  However real "Gun Folk" cringe at the miss use of the term Clip for Magazine.   See Clip definition 2 below.</I> <P><B>CLIP 2:</B> <I>A device used to rapidly load a magazine.  "Clip" is often used to refer to a magazine, but this is an improper use of the term.  There are two kinds of clips:  Stripper clips and en bloc clips:   <P>Stripper clips hold 5 to 10 rounds of ammunition by their bases.   To load the magazine, the clip is placed in a guide which is either a part of the gun, or a separate guide which slips onto the magazine.  Weapons which may be loaded from stripper clips include the Lee-Enfield series of rifles, Mosin-Nagant Rifles, the M1903 Springfield, and the Mauser 1898.  The Steyr-Hahn M1911 and Mauser "Broom Handle" semiautomatic pistols also use stripper clips.  Stripper clips are also called "chargers."<P>En bloc clips hold the cartridges together by their bases and their bodies; the clip and the rounds are inserted into the magazine as a unit.  When the last round is loaded, the clip is automatically ejected from the magazine.  Weapons loaded with en bloc clips include the Steyr-Mannlicher straight pull bolt action, the Mannlicher-Carcano rifles, and the US M1 Garand.  (In the M1, the clip is ejected up after the last round is fired.)</I><P><B>MAGAZINE:</B> <I>A spring-loaded container for cartridges that may be an integral part of the firearms  mechanism or may be detachable. Detachable magazines for the same model of firearm may be offered by the guns manufacturer or other manufacturers with various capacities. A gun with a five-shot detachable magazine, for instance, may be fitted with a magazine holding 10, 20, or 50 or more rounds. Box magazines are most commonly located under the receiver with the cartridges stacked vertically. Tube or tubular magazines typically run through the stock or under the barrel with the cartridges lying horizontally or end to nose.  Drum magazines hold their cartridges in a circular mode. The term magazine can also be used to describe a secure storage place for ammunition or explosives as in a war ships magazine.<P>The word Magazine may sound like a gun-related publication, but actually it is a small box, generally made of metal, that holds ammunition. Certain types of semiautomatic firearms are loaded by inserting a loaded magazine into the base of the firearm. Magazines are manufactured to hold a specific number of cartridges. A magazine is not a "clip", and use of the term "clip" to refer to a magazine (in spite of frequent TV goofs of this nature) is incorrect.  The Assault Weapons Ban of 1994, limited the magazine capacity of firearms produced after 1994 to a maximum of 10 cartridges. Old "Full Capacity"  and so called "High Capacity" magazines are still legal as long as they were manufacturered prior to the ban. Newly manufacturered full capacity magazines are marked "Restricted for Military and Law Enforcement Only."</I><P>Now that we have established that your weapon uses a magazine, what actually causes wear to the magazine follower spring is cycling. Loading and unloading the tension on the spring is what causes the spring steel to eventually fatigue and break down. It has been established time and again that keeping magazines loaded does not weaken the springs. Springs can remain compressed indefinitely without any adverse affect. I've seen articles were magazine were left loaded for over 50 years and still functioned without failure. <P>You may want to periodically disassemble your magazines to keep their internals clean and free from dirt, rust, or other corrosion that can shorten magazine and spring life, too.<P><p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||BN Boomer||12-21-2001||12:49 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>I was talking to some buddies yesterday about the "replace the shells after a year".. They had a good chuckle..      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh yeah, the old "My buddies said" defense....     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>All that proves is that your buddies are either as ignorant or misinformed about this subject as yourself or you have misrepresented to them what has thus far been said in this thread.<P>By the way, if rotating ammunition is such a bad idea, why does the Canadian military do it?<P><p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||BN Boomer||12-21-2001||01:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>BN: NICE AR-15 collection!!!<P>I'm assuming you know, but it is actually legal to own a fully automatic M-16 viariant (or other full-auto weapon) as a private citizen.<P>The only catch is quite a lot of BS and an FBI background check.  And if they find that you're a fellon, a nutcase, or just don't like your socks, they *can* deny you permission.<P>(wheras any 18 year old idoit can walk into wal-mart and buy a frigging .223 single...)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks, Ben. I understand all about the hoops that one must through to purchase Class III weapons on BATF Form 4s. I really love the Colt AR15s. If it were legal to own Class III weapons like full autos in Washington State, I would cough up the $6,000 plus that a pre-86 M16 costs in a heartbeat.<P>With all the crap that it takes to legally own a machine gun, they are the most well behaved class of firearms. They are only 2 or 3 instances of a legally owned machine gun being used in a crime in the last 70 years, and one of them involved a police officer. Yet even with that incredible low rate of incidence, our benevolent government (Democrats) saw fit to ban them through a  sneaky last minute amendment to the Firearms Owners Protection Act of 1986. As a result, an M16 that costs the government $750 new now costs us over $6,000 and is at least 15 years old. And people wonder why gun owners are so adamantly against registration.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||REDHOTT88||12-21-2001||01:29 PM||||Wampuscat..<P>I was responding to the fact that someone earlier said you can't give advice because you only have a few guns..<P>Yes, You SHOULD be well practiced in firearms, and yes, the more firearms you own AND shoot should help you gain that knowledge.<P>I know a World War II vet who has taught me and others so much about various firearms, it amazes me. He has shot every gun imaginable and is a NRA certified instructor. <P>However, he chooses to own only ONE 20 guage shotgun for an occasional partridge hunt.<P>Does that mean his opinion on guns is not acceptable or genuine?<P><BR>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">            <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||REDHOTT88||12-21-2001||05:34 PM||||This topic has turned into a P***ING contest..... I'm removing myself from it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><P>I'll let all the "experts" continue to post. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_zz.gif"><P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">           <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||13
