Z||000055||BN Boomer||12-23-2001||09:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Boomer: My apologies.. It sounded like the xbox was for you and you were jonesin...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, actually, I am kind of jonesin to play the thing.... Should be fun for the kids, too!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>but you do learn when you are constantly surrounded by it, and are being shown things constantly..<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, which between having grown up around guns and hunting, military service, and passionately pursuing the subject as an adult is how I came to gain most of my own knowledge about guns.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>But, ammo IS sealed.. Have you ever loaded a shell.. You have to press the bullet it, and you have to press the primer in. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Would you like me to post a pic of my Dillon RL550B progressive reloader, tumbler, primers I buy by the case, powder I buy in 8 pound kegs, or thousands upon thousands of cases and bullets I have laying around? Yeah, I might know thing or two about reloading....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Yes, you are correct about the basic mechanics of reloading. But you are incorrect in assuming that the friction alone the retains the bullet and primer is sufficient to provide an air and water-tight seal. This is why military and the highest quality commercial ammunition has an additional sealant applied, usually some form of lacquer. It can often be seen in the form of a reddish material. Even when the cartridge is sealed, the seal itself cannot protect against the effects of heat, thermal variation, and handling. This is why it is recommended that ammunition intended for use in applications that require the utmost reliability be periodically rotated.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000056||Phil||12-23-2001||10:46 PM||||Had to get in on this one finally. First my credentials, NRA certified instructor for over 30 yrs, Degree in Chem engr. Worked for Hercules at Radford Arsenal(solid fuel rocket motors and cannon powder manufacture), Capt. of pistol team and civilian instructor at the local Air Guard anti terrorist group, reloader for many years, big game hunter.<BR> Your 9mm ammo would most likely be fine, BUT for self defense ammo it would be best to replace it at least every year(you should practice much more often than that). You should also practice with the ammo that you will be using in the gun. If you are going to keep jhp in the gun then you should practice with that ammo. There have been cases where a gun will funtion perfectly with fmj but not with jhp. <BR>In the manufacture of gun powder acid is combined with the cotton and the nitrated cotton is neutralized and burning rate modifiers added. Over time residual acid can and does break down the gunpowder, storage conditions does affect this greatly. For a defense gun never take the chance- get a new box of ammo(same type) and shoot up the old. If you are not willing to put at least that much effort into it get rid of the firearm and use a baseball bat. I have seen too many people that have a gun and have no idea how to use it properly. Also if you have a gun for self defense, have you already asked yourself the question " Can I kill somebody?"  You had better know the answer to that question well before the situation arises. You cannot hesitate. Again practice practice practice - when the pucker factor comes into play it's very easy to miss even at 15 ft. <BR>Enough from me.||||reg||1
Z||000057||Fierowrecker||12-24-2001||08:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by reddog:<BR><B>Hey lets not forget a squib load! Thats when you pull the trigger and the gun fires but not as loud as usual. Meaning only the primer fired giving the round just enough velocity to travel out of the casing and into the chamber bore or barrel. Most people just think it is a misfire and eject the casing and reload and shot. The next thing you know your weapon is blowing up in your hand not a pretty picture. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Reddog!<BR>I had a squib with my nine...<BR>Fortunately, I looked through the barrel AFTER disassembly and saw the barrel was plugged...<BR>I had been using Blazer ammo, but now I never touch the stuff...<P>On a side note, always treat a firearm as if it IS loaded...<BR>Too many people have been shot with UNLOADED firearms...<P>I still don't understand the new law here in Michigan, no discharge of a firearm after sundown...<BR>I get most of my garden varments after sundown...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000058||Kasdan||12-24-2001||10:57 AM||||*walks in, sees 9mm's flying, runs out*||||reg||1
